\f1\fs22 Anthony \'93Chanta\'94 Chantara passed the hours of his existence without troubling himself whether his lot was truly a dog\'92s life, whether bullfighters could be ladies, whether he\'92d ever fall in love, whether his heart was wooden, or what colour suede shoes he was forbidden to step on. Chanta prided himself on his lack of thought or reflection, a near mental vacuum disturbed only by a persistent suspicion that the universe was forever poised to tax him over.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>There were advantages to scrubbing in an Airborne skyscraper, above the Layer: panoramic glass gave Chanta the full drama of the city, with clouds below poisonous enough to melt teeth, and swarms of hover-copts flitting between landing pads that sprouted from the sides of buildings like mushrooms on tall trees. The Airborne never descended to the ground or streets, and mid-air collisions between the plague of awkward flying machines were plentiful. A fact of life, and the reasonable price of avoiding the Cloudborne. The Airborne would rather roast in a fireball than breathe the same oxygen-starved air as their unworthy, under-Layer underlings.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Childhood Chanta, giddy and ten kinds of unsupervised, once watched two broken hover-copts spiral out of the Layer into the next apartment block, raining charred Gucci and incinerated optimism all over the gravel car park where he was playing rugby league with the neighbourhood kids using a punctured ball. He and his mates explored the crash site, laughed at the passengers\'92 lifeless grimaces\'97their lips burned away from their full-toothed Colgate grins\'97and traded jokes about the barbecue. It had filled his heart with righteous comfort.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta pushed his cleaning cart past condescending pretty faces floating above open-plan partitions. He paid their disapproving stares no mind; he was happy simply to jag work in an Airborne building that provided views other than out the windows, namely of beautiful, office-bred Airborne women. Over the years, his appreciation had given him to cataloguing them. Finance bred large breasts. Cyber Security engendered tall blondes. In Publishing, girls with endearing lisps flourished. Biotech waifs wore lab coats and irresistible come-fuck-me eyes. Events Management produced women who could organise the end of the world yet were incapable of cleaning their own toilets. Of all the vocations, Chanta shied away only from police and People & Culture consultants.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>At the far end of the floor, Chanta arrived at a window office, the scene of the crime. Yet another executive had opted for the suicide retirement package (please select between gun in mouth, necktie of computer cable, or overdose on your choice of restricted substance). Such retirements were on the rise. Not that anyone cared, especially the Department of Traffic Movement and Property Security, which begrudgingly passed for police in Ulyssia.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta resented the visceral mess of this executive\'92s resignation. Who used firearms these days? Particle-pulse weapons were quieter, more accurate and far cleaner, fusing and sealing all wounds. No bothersome rivers of blood\'97just drag the body out, give the floor a quick mop, and it\'92s back to business. But Travis Teh\'97his name still on door and desk plate\'97had used a truly messy, antiquated shotgun. His brains were gobbed on every surface.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>When Chanta was nearly finished, a tall, stunning blonde\'97
\f2\i must be in Cyber Security
\f1\i0 , he thought\'97strolled off the elevator, attracting a wake of slack-jawed gawkers of all genders from offices, meeting rooms, and around water coolers. At once, her blue eyes pierced Chanta to the core and kicked his heart into arrhythmia. Her high cheekbones, flawless skin, and faultless teeth drew the air from his lungs and knocked him aback. Her hair was a cascade of pure golden sand reflecting rainbows of colour.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Her legs, hips, and breasts\'97too small for Finance\'97made Chanta question his atheism.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Yet they approached ever closer; the woman\'85 no! Surely she wasn\'92t coming to talk to him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dimly reflected in the office\'92s glass wall, Chanta examined his grubby white overalls, and the large rip betraying a legacy of poor tailoring and an expanding butt. The rip branded him as among the grunts, no matter that he was wearing his good boxer shorts.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It took an age, then the woman was just outside the office. Looking at Chanta. His mind dredged for something witty and original to say, something whimsical yet pithy. As she entered, he nearly tripped over the couch.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93How long will you be?\'94 Her voice was joyously authoritarian. \'93I was told the office would be ready by now.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta tried to hide the rip in his overalls. \'93Shotgun,\'94 was all he could cough.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He tried again. Nothing. Then he wheezed. Worse.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah knew the effect she had on men, but she wouldn\'92t make it easy. 
\f2\i Men ain\'92t nothin\'92 but dicks and hormones
\f1\i0 , her aunt would say. No brains\'85 Sarah lost the thought, distracted by the cleaner rubbing his rump against the wall. Was he buffing away one last suicide smudge?\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Do you speak English?\'94 Sarah raised an ironic eyebrow, as she read the stitched label on the cleaner\'92s overalls: 
\fs18 ENCHANTED CLEANING SERVICES.
\fs22 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The cleaner gulped and rallied. \'93Yes, English. Mr Teh\'85 used a shotgun, miss.\'94 He smiled, as if completing a sentence was a personal Everest.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Shotgun?\'94 Sarah sounded predictably surprised. \'93Huh.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes miss.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah looked around the crime scene and crinkled her nose at the bloody office chair..\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92d recommend replacing that,\'94 he said, more relaxed. \'93Brains are a bastard to get out.\'94 He flushed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Are you hiding something nasty?\'94 Sarah asked, accusing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The question embarrassed him. \'93Miss?\'94 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You\'92ve been rigidly standing against the wall since I arrived. What are you hiding?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The cleaner\'92s embarrassment emerged as a weak grimace.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Step aside and let me see.\'94 She flicked her hand. The cleaner jumped aside, colliding with the glass wall.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah stifled a laugh as she circled her index finger as if stirring a drink. The cleaner turned slowly around. Sarah laughed when she saw the boxers through the rip in his overalls: pink, with cartoon fairies. In less mutant universes, the fairy would be recognised as Tinker Bell.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They were fifty percent off in a six-pack at Floormart,\'94 the cleaner explained, red-faced.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Are those places as bad as the streams I\'92ve seen?\'94 Sarah asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Floormarts? Worse. I was amazed I found a pack that was still sealed.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah straightened her spine and smile. \'93Well, next time I encounter splattered frontal lobes, I\'92ll know who to call. When will you be finished here?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Just have to steam-clean the carpet. You should be fine to settle in then.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Settle in? Oh, I\'92m not working in this slaughterhouse. It smells of cleaning fluids and diarrhoea, and\'85\'94 Sarah sniffed for toxic gases. \'93\'85 is that aftershave?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s, uh, vodka,\'94 the cleaner admitted, scratching a nasty shaving rash.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Cheap vodka,\'94 Sarah said, curling her nose.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah sat at her pristine desk, in her pristine high-back chair, in her gleaming new office, and stroked the fine-grained timber desktop with such satisfaction you\'92d think she\'92d inherited a gelato factory. Squaring her shoulders, she readied herself for business and powered up her quirky little holoscreen. An antique, but the peace it brought her\'85 well, that\'92s another story.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Its voice reported a table of messages, mostly from her old job. One was about her med-exam, 1:30pm today. Though a formality, promotions never came without a clean bill of health. Sarah had never been sick, never even had a cold. Viruses never touched her. If she ever sniffled, it was only due to the trace aromas of a shotgun suicide.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta\'92s thoughts dissolved into a cascade of fantasies. All he could feel was Sarah\'92s absence as a pain in his chest. He should steam-clean the carpet, but found himself stalking down the hallway, searching for her, his legs moving on their own. If only he could think of something\'97anything\'97to say.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ah, Mr Fairy Boxers,\'94 she said as he appeared at her door, hands clasped at his navel. \'93The scent of your vocation and vodka aftershave preceded you by several seconds.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta grasped for words until he saw her beloved holoscreen. \'93Still using a holoscreen?\'94 He chuckled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah rolled her eyes. Her expression was a bulletin in Womanese, unread by all men. Why do they think insults are good pick-up lines? No one disses her holoscreen.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What\'92s your name, Mr Fairy Boxers?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Tony Chantara, but people call me Chanta.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ah, I see. 
\f2\i Enchanted
\f1\i0  Cleaning Services. Very clever.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta blushed. \'93Well people say, I\'92m magic at clean-ups.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah look around the crime scene and crinkled her nose. \'93Well, you would have to be, I guess.\'94 She straightened and exhaled. \'93If you would excuse me, I have\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Do you like karaoke, miss?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s face froze. \'93What?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Karaoke. You know, people choosing to embarrass themselves with microphones and a backing track\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I know what karaoke is.\'94 Sarah shuddered, thinking of Johnny last night. \'93Fancy yourself a singer, Mr Fairy Boxers?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Tax, no! Not a musical bone in my body.\'94 Chanta smiled, hoping he had an in. \'93I like karaoke if someone else is singing.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah considered and nodded. \'93Good day, Mr Fairy Boxers.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta  became surly. \'93You know, I got the call at 4am for this gig, was told to ready the office for a new computer virtuoso. And what\'92s the thanks I get? Some blonde Cyber Security clone looks down her perfect nose at me. Maybe I should squirt Breezy Brain\'92s remains back into that room.\'94 He hoped Sarah might be nice, but he only felt shame at her abrupt dismissal. Typical Airborne: obscene riches, and they acted as if their bowels produced birthday cake. While he was taxed for life. Flourishing Cloudborne businesses were short-lived; they were either absorbed in hostile takeovers or crushed underfoot. Every morning, he woke fearing eviction and losing every scrap he owned.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah blinked. \'93Sorry if you found me rude, Mr Fairy Boxers. Is there anything else?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta set his jaw, puffed his chest. \'93You\'92re new here, eh?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Who told you that?\'94 she blurted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Miss, I\'92m not at liberty to discuss it,\'94 he said, enjoying putting Sarah at a disadvantage.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah leaned forward\'97her blouse gaping, giving a safe glimpse of lingerie. \'93Tell me how a cleaner knows anything about me.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>With pleasure souring, Chanta froze. After an eternity, he said, \'93I have a friend\'97well, more of a professional acquaintance. Never mind. He\'92s in Public Relations\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Which makes him a liar.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93\'97from Traffic Movement and Property Security\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oh, he\'92s the police! A colossal liar then.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93He\'92s in Homicide.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93A colossal homicidal liar.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I think calling him homicidal is a bit much.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Is he in Homicide or Public Relations? Public Relations and 
\f2\i homicidal
\f1\i0 , more likely.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta breezed past that. \'93The TRAMPS Commissioner always sends my friend to close high-profile cases. Whenever people die horribly, he directs the cleaning my way. That\'92s it, I swear. He just heard the new exec was a high-flyer.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>That was the last thing Sarah needed, a TRAMPS officer in homicide\'97or worse, public relations\'97taking an interest in her. Sarah\'92s eyes narrowed, suspecting cover-ups. \'93Do you enchant evidence away as well as you scrub away gore?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No?... No! Never,\'94 Chanta tried to keep from looking shifty. \'93I never tamper with evidence. I just get the job done.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes, and I should get my job done. Again, good day, Mr Fairy Boxers.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The elevator binged open. Chanta loaded it with his cart, his steam cleaner, and a discreetly bagged cargo of suicide goo.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>For Chanta, there was no "now or never"\'97just never. He felt like suicide goo; he and Travis Teh were equals. As fast as the elevator went, Chanta\'92s heart plummeted faster. As it dropped him, away from the office of angels, the dominion of Sarah\'97he grew lonelier. his gear and Teh\'92 remains\'97like so much disposable ballast\'97down the 216 storeys to the Cloudborne street, away from the office of angels, the dominion of Sarah\'97he grew lonelier. As fast as the elevator went, Chanta\'92s heart plummeted faster.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta bunted his cart out the service doors on the lower ground level of the Tower of Sarah, morosely and fatalistically whistling a tune that, in countless other universes, was a song called \'93Puppet on a String.\'94 In a spooky coincidence that hinted at the true nature of the multiverse, Chanta sang a line, censored here for the author\'92s legal security, of course.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Given enough free time, he may well have channelled the whole song, but his embittered thoughts surrendered to cursing the Airborne, whose engineered lack of care created the oppressively constant, wheezy Layer over his head which separated, both physically and symbolically, the privileged from the under-privileged, the entitled from the mis-titled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>One day, he mused, there\'92d be a revolution. An army of troubadours (matadors? pompadours?) would rise and\'85 what? Chanta could never decide. The voltage of his dim imagination fizzled out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He grunted, pushing the skidding cart up to street level, via a ramp glazed with condensed atmospheric chemicals. Later, he'd need to clean the wheels and axles sticky with the residue that gummed up everything below the Layer. The weather had been aggressive all week; his van would need its third wash to slow down its worsening corrosion.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta glanced up, as if he possessed cloud-piercing vision, as if he knew which windows were Sarah\'92s office, as if she was at those windows, basking in a halo of warm, glorious sunlight, looking beneficently down upon him. Near the van, before the Tower of Sarah vanished behind blocky Cloudborne architecture, Chanta paused for a last, forlorn look.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta would shortly be encountering Emil Kalheri\'92s expensively quoted corpse where it fell in the street, but not before Rick and Dick.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Rick and Dick took jobs nobody else wanted, the nastier the better. As part of what police and intelligence institutions somewhat casually referred to as \'93Independent Contracted Surveillance Ops\'94, they would break into houses to plant covert listening devices, hidden cameras, and, when opportunity knocked, the occasional bomb or hilarious booby trap. They took jobs nobody else wanted, the nastier the better. But the pay was dismal. That\'92s why they moonlighted as ambulance punks.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>When a call came in about a body in the street outside the Chapelle Boogie Bar, Rick radio-ed in for the job while Dick fired up their multipurpose surveillance van, currently disguised as a coronial pick-up unit. Once there, they saw no reason to hurry; their shift was hours away yet.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93He\'92s not going anywhere,\'94 Rick said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Parking illegally, they bought meat pies and coffee, then lounged in their tricked-out ambulance, delighted to watch the morning rush swirl indifferently around the corpse in the midnight blue suit, as stiff as public artwork. They hoped for a squeamish pedestrian to shriek, or, better yet, lose their breakfast, but the crowd stepped over and around the body with the practised apathy of hardened professionals.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>And then a miserable cleaner, arse hanging from ripped overalls, nearly bulldozed his cart over their target. When the cleaner tripped and landed square on the corpse, Rick and Dick cackled so hard they sprayed  flakes of pie and gobs of tomato sauce across their ambulance dashboard.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta didn\'92t see the obstacle on the pitted pavement. His cart jammed into a corpse dressed in an expensive, midnight blue suit. The cart jolted, threatening to topple; Chanta wrestled it back, lost balance, and sprawled over the dead man. He glanced around, mortified. No one cared. In the sabulous morning haze, passersby kept noses up and eyes straight, parting neatly around the tableau of clumsiness and death.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta, though not credited with brains, possessed a sharp, urchin cunning. For instance, this man wore an expensive, new off-black suit (Chanta would never know the colour was midnight blue, being unfamiliar with the colour of a crisp night sky above the Layer), yet the man\'92s hands were calloused, his fingers oddly bent, his nails filthy. Figuring there could be out-of-place valuables on the dead man\'92s person, and with the brazen face of a man living a largely invisible life, Chanta chiselled through the man\'92s pockets, shoes, and underwear.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Hey, you!\'94 barked a voice of petty authority behind his cart.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Up loomed a Coroner\'92s meat-wagon driver and a sullen off-sider.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I was just about to call you guys,\'94 Chanta simpered. \'93He\'92s been dead a while. Cold.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And how would you know he\'92s cold, mate?\'94 the off sider sniffed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I, uh\'85 I didn\'92t see him. I ran over him. With my cart.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93A cart with built-in heat sensors, eh?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No, I fell on him. Sorry.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yeah, yeah. Tax off, pal.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta taxed off. He vanished down three blocks to his illegally parked van in a dead-end alley. Behind the wheel, he checked his meagre haul\'97nothing more than a coin-sized chad of grainy, carbon-coloured leather. In Chanta\'92s fingers, the disc was stiffly flexible; it sprang slovenly back into shape when he stopped bending it. For a moment, he thought he saw it flash a rainbow of colours. He waited to catch it again, but it stayed inert\'97the Layer often played tricks with light. He shrugged and tossed the trinket on the passenger-side floor and coaxed the van\'92s motor into life.
